WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.960
"Animal Protein vs. Plant-Based Protein"

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:14.000
In 2019, Dr. David Katz
and colleagues—

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.480
including one of my favorite
researchers, David Jenkins—

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.200
published a public health
case for modernizing

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.920
the definition of protein quality.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.440
The prevailing definition seems to
have more to do with biochemistry

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.840
than the net effects on human health.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.040
The popular concept that protein
is good, and the more the better,

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.800
coupled with a protein quality
definition that favors animal protein,

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.680
fosters the impression that eating
more meat, eggs, and dairy

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.440
is desirable and preferable,
but this is directly opposed

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.800
to nutrition guidelines that are
instead trying to push more plants.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.320
Although protein malnutrition is still
prevalent in many areas of the world,

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:51.920
it is exceedingly rare
in the industrialized world,

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:53.920
where the most formidable
public health threat

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:57.120
is not something like kwashiorkor—
protein/calorie malnutrition—

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.760
but from chronic diseases.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.800
In 2016, a landmark study
was published out of Harvard,

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:07.520
involving more than 100,000 people,
that found that replacing animal protein

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:12.320
with plant protein was associated
with lower risk of dying prematurely.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:15.600
The worst seemed to be
processed meat like bacon,

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.920
as well as egg protein,
the egg whites,

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:22.560
but swapping in even just 3% plant
protein for any of the animal proteins—

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.680
processed meat, unprocessed meat,
chicken, fish, eggs, or dairy—

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.920
was associated with a
significantly lower risk

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:32.240
of arguably the most important
endpoint of all: death.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.640
Yeah, but how do we
know it's the protein?

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:37.840
The researchers adjusted for factors
such as saturated fat intake,

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:41.040
which suggested it wasn't
just the animal fat.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.120
Okay, but how does your body
even know the difference

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:45.920
between protein from a plant
and protein from an animal?

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:47.200
Isn't protein, protein?

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:50.960
No. Unlike animal protein,
plant protein is generally low

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:53.120
in branched-chain
amino acids, for example,

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.760
and decreased consumption
of branched-chain amino acids

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:58.560
improves metabolic health.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:00.000
It could be the IGF-1,

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.400
a cancer-promoting growth
hormone that is boosted

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:05.760
by so-called high-quality
animal protein intake, though.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.640
We suspect the IGF-1
connection is cause and effect,

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.920
since people who are born
to have higher IGF-1 levels

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:14.000
regardless of what they eat
do appear to suffer

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.480
higher rates of killers like
type 2 diabetes and heart disease.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.360
Or it could be something the
Harvard researchers didn't control for

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:24.240
such as toxic pollutants like
as dioxins and PBCs,

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:27.440
since they tend to accumulate
up the food chain in cattle,

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:31.360
pigs, chickens, and fish,
and therefore end up on our plates.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.920
So plant-based protein also
stands as an important step

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.600
to lower the body burden
of harmful pollutants.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:40.080
If you don't think 100,000
people are enough,

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:41.920
how about 400,000 people?

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:47.440
The NIH-AARP study is the largest
diet cohort study in history.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:50.880
And again, simply swapping
3% of calories from various

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:53.280
animal protein sources
with plant protein

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:56.720
was associated with 10%
decreased overall mortality,

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:01.600
and you get even twice that benefit
if you get rid of eggs, too.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:05.200
That's not a surprise, since
egg consumption is associated

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:10.000
with a higher risk of developing
cardiovascular disease.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:13.920
Put all the studies together on
dietary protein intake and mortality,

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:17.600
and people who eat more protein
tend to live shorter lives,

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:21.680
but this is mainly driven by the
harmful association of animal protein.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:25.360
Plant protein intake is inversely
associated with mortality,

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:29.360
meaning those who eat more plant
protein tend to live longer lives.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:32.080
More animal protein
may mean more mortality,

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:35.280
whereas more plant protein
is correlated with less mortality.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:37.760
So the best of both worlds
would be to increase

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:41.200
the intake of plant protein
in place of animal protein.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:45.440
In other words, as another
2020 meta-analysis concluded,

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:48.400
"Persons should be encouraged to
increase their plant protein intake

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:52.355
to potentially decrease
their risk of death."

